<section class="block">
<h2>What changed</h2>
<p>Anthropic made its managed Claude Platform generally available on Google Cloud on 22 October 2026: Google Cloud customers can use the full platform with Google Cloud IAM credentials, billing and commitment retirement<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Coverage notes that new platform features arrive on the same day as Anthropic's own API<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, that Anthropic is the data processor for this option while Google remains the processor for the existing Google-managed option, and that it launched with US and global endpoints<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why the route matters, not just the model</h2>
<p>For a long time the choice was "which model". Now, for the same model, the route you buy it through changes several things leaders care about: which company processes your data under contract, which regions are available, how quickly new capabilities reach your teams, and which commitments the spend counts against.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Your apps</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Products and agents<em>call one internal API</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Gateway</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Model gateway<em>routing, policy, logging, budgets</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Routes</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Model maker, direct<em>its own platform</em></span><span class="hl">Model maker via cloud<em>maker processes data, cloud bills</em></span><span>Cloud-managed<em>cloud processes data</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Decide by</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Data processor<em>contract and DPA</em></span><span>Region<em>residency rules</em></span><span>Feature timing<em>same day or later</em></span><span>Commitments<em>where spend counts</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>A gateway keeps the route a configuration choice, so you can use different routes for different workloads.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How to decide</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Classify</b><span>Which workloads touch personal or regulated data, and where</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Processor</b><span>Which company may process that data under your contracts</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Region</b><span>Whether the route offers the endpoints your rules require</span></li>
<li><b>Features</b><span>Whether same-day access matters for this workload</span></li>
<li><b>Commercials</b><span>Commitments, discounts and billing consolidation</span></li>
<li><b>Route</b><span>Configure per workload in the gateway, and review quarterly</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Compliance questions first, convenience second.</figcaption>
</figure>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Why it matters</th><th>Where it bites</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Who is the data processor?</td><td>Determines contracts, audits and sub-processor lists</td><td>Regulated sectors everywhere</td></tr>
<tr><td>Which endpoints are offered?</td><td>Residency rules may require specific regions</td><td>EU, India, Saudi Arabia and other markets with localisation expectations</td></tr>
<tr><td>How fast do features arrive?</td><td>Agent and tool features can unblock products</td><td>Teams building agents and coding tools</td></tr>
<tr><td>Where does spend count?</td><td>Cloud commitments can make one route cheaper in practice</td><td>Organisations with large committed spend</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Separate who decides what</h2>
<p>The cleanest organisations separate concerns: a platform team owns the gateway and the approved routes, and application teams choose from those routes without negotiating contracts themselves. Kubernetes networking made the same separation explicit, with infrastructure providers, cluster operators and application developers each owning their own resources<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/claude-google-cloud-anthropic-managed-choice/roles.webp" alt="Diagram of Gateway API resources: infrastructure providers own GatewayClass, cluster operators own Gateway, and application developers in separate namespaces own their HTTPRoutes and Services" width="1000" height="595" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Role-oriented ownership: providers, platform operators and application teams each own one layer. The same split works for model access. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2021/04/22/evolving-kubernetes-networking-with-the-gateway-api/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Gateway API authors</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<div class="callout"><p>Buy models through the route your compliance needs, not the one that is easiest to sign. A gateway lets you hold several routes without your applications noticing.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>This is a welcome option for Google Cloud shops that want the full Claude Platform without a separate procurement, and the same-day feature access matters for teams building agents. The decision that deserves the most care is the processor and region question: check your data processing agreements, sub-processor lists and residency requirements before moving regulated workloads, especially outside the US (<a href="/regions/">by region</a>). Whatever you choose, keep a gateway in front so the route stays a configuration choice; see <a href="/insights/llm-model-routing/">LLM model routing</a>, <a href="/case-studies/ai-provider-resilience-cps-230-insurer/">AI provider resilience</a> and <a href="/insights/build-vs-buy-ai-capabilities/">build versus buy for AI capabilities</a>.</p>
</section>

